"Show us what you’re building with the ChatGPT Apps SDK 🚀 - #73 by derek_ding - ChatGPT Apps SDK - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/show-us-what-you-re-building-with-the-chatgpt-apps-sdk/1365862/73)\nciteturn30966search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: last month; derek_ding January 17, 2026, 7:51am 73 ... Github: AI-Hub-Admin/fortune-compass-agent\n\n# Show us what you’re building with the ChatGPT Apps SDK 🚀\n\nChatGPT Apps SDK\n\nderek_ding January 17, 2026, 7:51am 73\n\nOne-liner\nThe Fortune Compass App can tell your fortune by Tarot Cards, Zhouyi (I Ching), GuanGong and many cultural divination methods.\n\nWho it’s for / problem it solves\n\nPeople who are interested in fortune-telling, and would like to seek advice for their career, future, happiness, relationship and family and more.\n\nKey features:\n\ntell_fortune: After user asked a question \"What will my career be like this Year? \". The tool can actually randomly draw a card from 78 Tarot Cards, 1 symbol from 64 Zhouyi (I Ching) symbols and the Guangong oracles, and pass\nthe drawn cards such as “the-fool” card from Tarot or “Qian” (111111) from Zhouyi and the drawed results and meaning to LLM. And the LLM and reason and help you read the sign can give you advices, which is very similar to how you\nactually do with Tarot ot Zhouyi, etc.\n\nimage1686×1308 234 KB\n\nStack & tools:\nMCP: Node MCP 1.24 version, Express\nHost and Deploy: aiagenta2z\n\nLink(s):\nMCP Server URL:\nGithub: AI-Hub-Admin/fortune-compass-agent\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat is the difference between image generation and output of image tokens? - API - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/what-is-the-difference-between-image-generation-and-output-of-image-tokens/1371851)\nciteturn30966search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; idealpyj January 17, 2026, 3:18am 1 ... Model  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n\n# What is the difference between image generation and output of image tokens?\n\nAPI\n\napi, image-generation\n\nidealpyj January 17, 2026, 3:18am 1\n\nThe pricing standards for image generation models include two categories: Image tokens and Image generation. While the input pricing within Image tokens is straightforward, what confuses me is the difference between Image generation and output within Image tokens.\n\n_j January 17, 2026, 4:07am 2\n\nThe output is made of image tokens.\n\nQuality  | Square (1024×1024)  | Portrait (1024×1536)  | Landscape (1536×1024)\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nLow  | 272 tokens  | 408 tokens  | 400 tokens\nMedium  | 1056 tokens  | 1584 tokens  | 1568 tokens\nHigh  | 4160 tokens  | 6240 tokens  | 6208 tokens\n\nThese are billed at the output image token price for the model, resulting in an output cost:\n\n### Per-image output pricing by size (n=1)\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1.5  | Low  | $0.008704  | $0.013056  | $0.012800\n | Medium  | $0.033792  | $0.050688  | $0.050176\n | High  | $0.133120  | $0.199680  | $0.198656\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1  | Low  | $0.01088  | $0.01632  | $0.01600\n | Medium  | $0.04224  | $0.06336  | $0.06272\n | High  | $0.16640  | $0.24960  | $0.24832\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1 Mini  | Low  | $0.002176  | $0.003264  | $0.003200\n | Medium  | $0.008448  | $0.012672  | $0.012544\n | High  | $0.033280  | $0.049920  | $0.049664\n\n### Output pricing per 1000 images by size (1k)\n\n  * in dollars and cents for easier understanding\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1.5  | Low  | $8.71  | $13.06  | $12.80\n | Medium  | $33.80  | $50.69  | $50.18\n | High  | $133.12  | $199.68  | $198.66\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1  | Low  | $10.88  | $16.32  | $16.00\n | Medium  | $42.24  | $63.36  | $62.72\n | High  | $166.40  | $249.60  | $248.32\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1 Mini  | Low  | $2.18  | $3.27  | $3.20\n | Medium  | $8.45  | $12.68  | $12.55\n | High  | $33.28  | $49.92  | $49.67\n\n# Input\n\nNote that image input (for the image AI to look at), in a chat with the image tool called or images as input on the edits endpoint, can cost you $0.035 to $0.067 per image on gpt-image-1.5, or on gpt-image-1 when using “input_fidelity”: “high” - the big surprise pricing.\n\nidealpyj January 17, 2026, 8:20am 3\n\nThank you for your response; it has been immensely helpful to me.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNext-Generation Conversational AI: A Design-Proposal Approach - #22 by CatLoverSachiAndKei - Use cases and examples - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/next-generation-conversational-ai-a-design-proposal-approach/1371568/22)\nciteturn30966search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; CatLoverSachiAndKei January 17, 2026, 7:37am 22\n\n# Next-Generation Conversational AI: A Design-Proposal Approach\n\nChatGPT Use cases and examples\n\nchatgpt, prompt-design, chatgpt-ux\n\nCatLoverSachiAndKei January 17, 2026, 7:37am 22\n\nHi everyone,\n\nI’d like to share a small design thought.\n\nThere is a disciplined robot—an AI-equipped robot that can act autonomously.\n\nWhen we introduce a relational design concept like ECS-AI V2, the system starts to feel different. It becomes a relationship-oriented autonomous conversational robot.\nNow, the AI does more than produce correct answers or efficient actions. It also considers the continuity of interaction and behavior—for example, keeping track of previous topics when a user switches questions, adjusting tone during long conversations, tailoring responses based on prior interactions, and choosing actions that respect the user’s context—to maintain a stable and comfortable relationship with the user over time.\n\nThis small progression shows how adding a relational layer can shift a system from pure autonomy toward relationally aware, conversational behavior.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Official ImageGen, 4o and Dall-E Megathread - #1783 by Tina_ChiKa - Community - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/the-official-imagegen-4o-and-dall-e-megathread/1230134/1783)\nciteturn30966search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; Tina_ChiKa January 17, 2026, 6:38pm 1783\n\n# The Official ImageGen, 4o and Dall-E Megathread\n\nCommunity\n\ndalle3, gallery, gpt-image-1, gpt-4o-images, gpt-image-15\n\nTina_ChiKa January 17, 2026, 6:38pm 1783\n\nfile_00000000b320720a99dca88ed612b5181536×1024 302 KB\n\nHere is mine Image: :cherry_blossom:\n\nIndeed, a mighty prompt do you have Image: :face_with_monocle:Image: :mechanical_arm:Image: :+1:\n\nImage windysoliloquy:\n\n> No judgment if you have anime kitties in your context,\n\nwell, is it really context-dependent?\n\nA quick reminder:\n\nWhen generating images, you are interacting with two models – directly and indirectly.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSIP calls fail for a few minutes every day - Bugs - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/sip-calls-fail-for-a-few-minutes-every-day/1371777)\nciteturn30966search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; Could you please investigate whether any recent changes or updates on the OpenAI side might be contributing to these intermittent connection issues?Sean-Der January 17, 2026, 3:57am 3\ns-nishihara1 January 16, 2026, 9:39am 2\n\nWe’re also using the SIP calling integration to automate phone-based customer support in Japan. Over the past week, we’ve seen an increase in connection failures during specific, short windows (a few minutes at a time), though service typically recovers quickly afterward.\nCould you please investigate whether any recent changes or updates on the OpenAI side might be contributing to these intermittent connection issues?\n\nSean-Der January 17, 2026, 3:57am 3\n\nHi!\n\nSorry about this. We had some bugs internally, but they got fixed today! Realtime API unreliable over SIP - #13 by Sean-Der\n\n  * The 0 second calls were because the webhook service had OOMed (so we failed right away)\n  * High Latency/Lag was because of internal delay\n\nIf you continue to see issues please @ me or send me an email. I am really trying to get this service in a better spot. Thanks for the patience.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nError using response_format for json-output? - API - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/error-using-response-format-for-json-output/1371890)\nciteturn30966search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; rapid1898 January 17, 2026, 8:02pm 1 ...     client = OpenAI(api_key = CHATGPT_API_KEY) ... This forum post has discussion about using the server-side chat history, but the code snippet at the bottom is a fully-realized use of responses.parse() for sending in a pydantic basemodel as a text.format and the API SDK library validating and loading the output to a “parsed” alongside “content”. ... Since mid-2023 models, you also could write that instruction about always producing JSON and turn on text.format → “type”: “json_object” and get some more training in producing JSON.\n\n# Error using response_format for json-output?\n\nAPI\n\nrapid1898 January 17, 2026, 8:02pm 1\n\nHello - i try to read a txt-file, analyse some data and ouput the result in json-format using the following code:\n    \n    `import os, sys\n    from urllib.parse import urlparse\n    import time\n    from dotenv import load_dotenv\n    from openai import OpenAI\n    from pydantic import BaseModel\n    from typing import List\n    \n    class ArticleSummary(BaseModel):\n        category: str\n    class Messages(BaseModel):\n        messages: List[ArticleSummary]\n    \n    path = os.path.abspath(os.path.dirname(sys.argv[0])) \n    \n    fn = os.path.join(path, \".env\")\n    load_dotenv(fn)\n    CHATGPT_API_KEY = os.environ.get(\"CHATGPT_API_KEY\")\n    client = OpenAI(api_key = CHATGPT_API_KEY)\n    \n    fn = os.path.join(path, \"prompt.txt\")\n    with open(fn, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n      lines = f.read().splitlines()\n    question = \"\\n\".join(lines)\n    \n    ### upload txt-file for analysis\n    fn = os.path.join(path, \"categories.txt\")\n    time.sleep(5)  \n    `\n\nUnfortunately i get this erroor when running the program:\n\n`TypeError: Responses.create() got an unexpected keyword argument ‘response_format’`\n\nHow can i output the result as json-file?\n\n_j January 17, 2026, 10:07pm 2\n\nYou have several missteps there in forming the correct API call.\n\nThis forum post has discussion about using the server-side chat history, but the code snippet at the bottom is a fully-realized use of responses.parse() for sending in a pydantic basemodel as a text.format and the API SDK library validating and loading the output to a “parsed” alongside “content”. You can still consume the API SDK helper’s `response.output_text` to receive and save the plain text of the JSON response.\n\nImage\n\nDoes response chaining with previous_response_id only work with text response type and not json_schema or structured response type? API\n\n> You can have a conversation context with a chain of previous response IDs when using “store”:true on the Responses API, even if the output is a JSON object specified by a text.format for structured output, even strict. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat is the difference between image generation and output of image tokens? - #2 by _j - API - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/what-is-the-difference-between-image-generation-and-output-of-image-tokens/1371851/2)\nciteturn30966search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; _j January 17, 2026, 4:07am 2 ... Model  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n\n_j January 17, 2026, 4:07am 2\n\nThe output is made of image tokens.\n\nQuality  | Square (1024×1024)  | Portrait (1024×1536)  | Landscape (1536×1024)\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nLow  | 272 tokens  | 408 tokens  | 400 tokens\nMedium  | 1056 tokens  | 1584 tokens  | 1568 tokens\nHigh  | 4160 tokens  | 6240 tokens  | 6208 tokens\n\nThese are billed at the output image token price for the model, resulting in an output cost:\n\n### Per-image output pricing by size (n=1)\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1.5  | Low  | $0.008704  | $0.013056  | $0.012800\n | Medium  | $0.033792  | $0.050688  | $0.050176\n | High  | $0.133120  | $0.199680  | $0.198656\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGPT Image 1  | Low  | $0.01088  | $0.01632  | $0.01600\n | Medium  | $0.04224  | $0.06336  | $0.06272\n | High  | $0.16640  | $0.24960  | $0.24832\n\nModel  | Quality  | 1024×1024  | 1024×1536  | 1536×1024\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAGENTS.md File Optimization - Codex - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/agents-md-file-optimization/1369152)\nciteturn30966search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; You want to put things that are “unexpected”, surprising or otherwise unknowable to the AI model. ... Also, structure your agents files so that they contextually give relevant information- in most agent frameworks, `AGENTS.md` (or `agents.md`) files are typically discovered by walking up the directory tree and applied sequentially. ... whafa January 17, 2026, 6:16am 3\n\nYou want to put things that are “unexpected”, surprising or otherwise unknowable to the AI model. The model has read the internet. It already knows this stuff- you just need to tell it that’s what you want.\n\nWhat it can’t know is what your github username is, or that you use Bazel not Make, or that you prefer all projects to be in your ~/development subdirectory, or you want 80%+ code coverage. Tell it your preferences and requirements.\n\nAlso, structure your agents files so that they contextually give relevant information- in most agent frameworks, `AGENTS.md` (or `agents.md`) files are typically discovered by walking up the directory tree and applied sequentially. So your root agent file should talk about things that are generally applicable to the whole company/repo, the leaf nodes should talk about project specific topics.\n\nI’d guess that most of that generated AGENTS.md file is just burning tokens- it WILL give you some degree of consistency, but you’d probably get most of that anyway if the conventions already exist in your repo.\n\nwhafa January 17, 2026, 6:16am 3\n\nThank you for taking the time to make that reply! I haven’t had to think about my token burn very much until just recently, but your words are particularly apt right now!--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRequest a new feature for ChatGPT - #157 by bokespain - Feature requests - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/request-a-new-feature-for-chatgpt/41624/157)\nciteturn30966search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; bokespain January 17, 2026, 3:07am 157Hello OpenAI team,\n\nbokespain January 17, 2026, 3:07am 157\n\nHello OpenAI team,\n\nI’d like to suggest a workflow improvement for Custom GPTs that would significantly reduce friction when managing lots of chats.\n\nFeature request:\n\n  * Add an optional capability that allows a Custom GPT to set (or at least reliably suggest with a one-click “Apply”) the chat title, driven by the GPT’s Instructions.\n\n  * Example instruction concept: “Set chat title to: — ”, where the GPT extracts those fields from the user’s first message (or from provided text).\n\n  * If the GPT cannot confidently extract the fields, it should ask a single short question and then set the title.\n\n  * Provide a fallback: if is missing, title becomes just .\n\nWhy this matters:\n\n  * Renaming chats manually is repetitive and error-prone, especially when running many conversations that follow the same pattern.\n\n  * Title automation would make chats far easier to organise and retrieve, particularly inside Projects.\n\nThanks for considering this improvement.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGpt-image-1.5 decreased image render quality? - API - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/gpt-image-1-5-decreased-image-render-quality/1371885)\nciteturn30966search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; jeffvpace January 17, 2026, 7:16pm 3 ... The API should be relatively stable, but with the image models, OpenAI has a three-year pattern of modifying the same model name and the quality delivered also.\n\n# Gpt-image-1.5 decreased image render quality?\n\nAPI\n\ngpt-image-15\n\nVivo_Properties January 17, 2026, 5:50pm 1\n\nI have noticed a significant increase in artifacts in gpt-image-1.5-2025-12-16 when using the endpoint for editing. To investigate this, I re-ran the prompt and rerendered the same images i had rendered in DEC, only to find that the quality has decreased considerably. Specifically, during editing, there are numerous artifacts, the text on packaging is no longer rendered correctly, and the dimensions are distorted.\n\nHas there been a change that has reduced the model’s performance?\n\nVivo_Properties January 17, 2026, 6:32pm 2\n\nGPT REPLIED\n\nShort answer\n\nThere has not been an intentional downgrade of image quality, but there have been changes in the editing pipeline and safety constraints that can negatively affect certain high-precision edit use cases, especially ones like yours.\n\nWhy December renders look better\n\nYour December images benefited from:\n\n  * Less aggressive edit safety\n\n  * Higher tolerance for text fidelity\n\n  * A closer “copy-then-modify” behavior during edits\n\nRe-running the same prompt today does not recreate the same internal conditions.\n\njeffvpace January 17, 2026, 7:16pm 3\n\n> Has there been a change that has reduced the model’s performance?\n\nI create image edits all the time using gpt-image-1.5 and this is not my experience. Then again, I don’t use text much…\n\nCan you provide an example so that you could be better assisted?\n\n(1) Provide the original image (2) Provide the edited version (3) Provide the prompt\n\nAlso, it would be helpful if you could provide one of your DEC images for comparison.\n\njeffvpace January 17, 2026, 7:22pm 4\n\nCan you provide a screen shot of that conversation?\n\nVivo_Properties January 18, 2026, 11:53am 5\n\nORIGINAL - TO EDIT\n\nIMG_20251230_1324401920×1440 524 KB\n\nDEC RENDER - PERFECT! TOP MODEL!!\n\nJANUARY RENDER - EXACTLY SAME PROMPT - THE IMAGE IS GARBAGE - NO COMMERCIAL VALUE AT ALL…\n\nRemote controls artifact\nFeet of the coffee table, a total mess.\nOriginal table had four wooden legs → enhanced gets a single black leg in the center.\n\nPillows on the couch - awful\n\nZero reliability - Zero commercial value.\n\nWe have dozens of other examples comparing renderings from December to those created yesterday 17/JAN. The results are awful and we can provide them upon request.\n\nWe trusted the model and OpenAI, and built an entire application on top of it. However, OpenAI changed the model parameters without any notice, and the results are no longer reliable. It was a mistake to invest time and money into developing this product based on this company. This lack of transparency could damage the company’s reputation and credibility. These are serious matters, and I cannot imagine how they could happen. These are not the tactics of a serious company.\n\nVivo_Properties January 18, 2026, 11:54am 6\n\nDEC RENDER - PERFECT\n\ndec_render_accurate_perfect_remotecotrols_coffeetable927×615 126 KB\n\nVivo_Properties January 18, 2026, 11:56am 7\n\nJAN RENDER - GARBAGE\n\njan_17_render_innacurate_coffeetable_remotecontrols1121×756 207 KB\n\nVivo_Properties January 18, 2026, 11:57am 8\n\nGPT SCREENSHOT\n\nimage1392×848 70.7 KB\n\n_j January 18, 2026, 12:02pm 9\n\nReal answer, not from AI: this topic is in the wrong category.\n\nChatGPT is a consumer product. OpenAI doesn’t name “gpt-image-1.5” there. You get the “make pictures with ChatGPT” with whatever facility OpenAI wants to deliver.\n\nVivo_Properties January 18, 2026, 12:12pm 10\n\nSo that means:\n\n  1. Model can be changed under the hood with no transparency?\n  2. API is useless - model is only for consumer use right?\n\nChatGPT a consumer product? haha\n\n_j January 18, 2026, 12:33pm 11\n\nAre you pointlessly asking ChatGPT? That’s what your screenshot shows. Is ChatGPT where you are asking for images also? Or are you truly making API calls, specifying the model, the size, and the output quality parameter?\n\nThe API should be relatively stable, but with the image models, OpenAI has a three-year pattern of modifying the same model name and the quality delivered also.\nYou only need to send a request made three years ago to DALL-E 2 and see junk come out of that model today, so there’s definitely not a hands-off policy on the API either.\n\nVeitB January 18, 2026, 12:37pm 12\n\nHi,\n\nTo help me better understand the issue, are you generating images through ChatGPT or via the API?\n\nWe have not seen similar reports so far, but it would still be useful to understand what might be driving the observed change in performance.\n\nVivo_Properties January 18, 2026, 12:50pm 13\n\nImage Vivo_Properties:\n\n> I have noticed a significant increase in artifacts in gpt-image-1.5-2025-12-16 when using the endpoint for editing.\n\nThat’s was my initial post.\n\nWhat kind of question is that guys? Did you think we use the ChatGPT chat, we have built an ai real estate app for image enhancement.\n\nOf course we use the API. Specifically, we use the endpoint that is dedicated to edits.\n\n/v1/images/edits\n\nVeitB January 18, 2026, 1:13pm 14\n\nThanks for confirming. I’ll keep an eye open for more reports.\nI asked because we have seen this time and time again in the forum.\n\n_j January 18, 2026, 2:33pm 15\n\nYou will notice one peculiar artifact with gpt-image-1.5 on the edits endpoint: the more you iterate, the more the image devolves into a blotchy mess of globs. Like someone painting with a wet sponge dabbed all over the image. There is no more “look at the person change race over 200 trials”, there is \"look at the literal watermarks make a fake texture all over.\n\njeffvpace January 18, 2026, 3:06pm 16\n\nImage Vivo_Properties:\n\n> OpenAI changed the model parameters without any notice\n\nWhat parameters are you referring to?\n\nAlso, what was your image edit prompt for the ORIGINAL to DEC RENDER?\n\nHere is my edit of your ORIGINAL:\n\nPrompt: Recreate the image: Increase the brightness by 3%.\n\nimage21536×1024 260 KB\n\nYou should realize that image edits are not perfect and are heavily influenced by the prompt - and gpt-image-1.5 is very good with following prompt instructions.\n\nVivo_Properties January 18, 2026, 4:53pm 17\n\nWhen I refer to parameters, I mean internal functions rather than the settings we can adjust. The prompt we are using is 638 words, I can’t post it here. We developed it step by step, testing it on many photos of Airbnb listings until we achieved a perfect result - based on DEC renderings.\n\nThank you very much for the image and the prompt you sent. I used your prompt with the edit endpoint, which gave me the following result using a low setting which again has some artifacts - for example the pillows. When I increase the setting to medium, artifacts begin to appear. Which endpoint did you use? The 1/images/generations or v1/images/edits?\n\nResult:\nRecreate the image: Increase the brightness by 3%. @ v1/images/edits - low\n\nimage1536×1024 231 KB\n\njeffvpace January 18, 2026, 5:07pm 18\n\nImage Vivo_Properties:\n\n> Which endpoint did you use?\n\n…v1/images/edits\n\n“quality“ = “high“\n\n“input_fidelity” = “high”\n\nResults and comparisons will vary - of course…\n\n_j January 18, 2026, 5:20pm 19\n\nImage Vivo_Properties:\n\n> The prompt we are using is 638 words…\n> \n> v1/images/edits - low\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImage/File become corrupted upon upload with ChatGPT 30-days free trial - Bugs - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/image-file-become-corrupted-upon-upload-with-chatgpt-30-days-free-trial/1371886)\nciteturn30966search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; lea0130 January 17, 2026, 5:53pm 1 ... I was on GPT 5.2, but at the top left corner of your chat, you can switch the model that your AI uses, and I switched mine to the GPT-4o legacy model.\n\n# Image/File become corrupted upon upload with ChatGPT 30-days free trial\n\nChatGPT Bugs\n\nchatgpt, bug, chatgpt-plus\n\nlea0130 January 17, 2026, 5:53pm 1\n\nYesterday on January 16th, 2026 I was offered a ChatGPT 30-days free trial which I accepted.\n\nNote: The offer was real because I got it directly on the official chatgpt[.]com webpage when I was about to ask ChatGPT something and I also got a confirmation mail for it. (Not that anyone thinks I fell for a scam or something)\n\nSince then ChatGPT tells me that it cannot view or read any images or files that I uploaded and strangely enough, this happened after the exact same amount of images and files that I can also upload with the free plan.\n\nI can confidently confirm this because I tried to upload the exact same images with another ChatGPT account of mine that uses the free plan and there it could view or describe the images that I’ve uploaded.\n\nAfter the amount that I normally can also upload with the free plan, it began to tell me:\n\nThe result of my free plan account after uploading the exact same Reddit .png meme: imgur[.]com/a/8iKgei4\n\nSeaella January 18, 2026, 5:34pm 2\n\nThis is happening to me too, please let me now if a fix is found\n\nFriendlypal January 20, 2026, 9:56am 3\n\nThis has been happening to me for the past week. I opened a support ticket but have been back and forth with their support guy who’s not been that helpful so far. Asking for a video upload of the issue. Send it. Sorry we can’t accept 3rd party sharing services (wetransfer), try upload it to the help centre. I can’t send videos through the help centre as it only allows image uploads. No response for days. No images work still. It’s a fault their end as I’ve tested everything and gpt can even tell me what size the image is and all its properties but when asked, says the image is just of a repeating string of characters. I’d encourage people to complain through help centre as well as on here until they figure out why it’s decoding wrong.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFile input parameters to tools - API - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/file-input-parameters-to-tools/1371839)\nciteturn30966search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; If a param is listed under `openai/fileParams`, the model will always pass a string file ID. ... Jaime_Cham January 17, 2026, 2:39am 2\nOpenAI Developer Community\n\n# File input parameters to tools\n\nJaime_Cham January 16, 2026, 7:59pm 1\n\nIn Build your MCP server it is specified that tool input properties that receive file inputs should be specified as:\n```\n    \n    `    imageToProcess: {\n          type: \"object\",\n          properties: {\n            download_url: { type: \"string\" },\n            file_id: { type: \"string\" },\n          },\n          required: [\"download_url\", \"file_id\"],\n          additionalProperties: false,\n        },\n    `\n\nHowever, when I do this, ChatGPT refuses to call that tool (it fails silently). When I asked ChatGPT why it cannot call that tool it says that I defined it as taking a `string`. Further questioning has it telling me this is by design (see attached transcript below).\n\nSo this is something permanent we have to work around to? should this be documented somewhere if so?\n\nThanks!\n\n\nShort answer: this discrepancy is expected behavior, and it’s coming from the Actions tool normalization layer, not from you sending the definition “wrong”.\n\n> “Change `edit_video` to accept file_0 objects”\n\nImage: :cross_mark: will never work at invocation time, even if the schema allows it.\n\n* * *\n\n## Image: :white_check_mark: The correct mental model\n\nConcern  | What to rely on\n--- | ---\nUI / widgets  | `{ file_id, download_url }`\nTool invocation  | `string` (file_id)\nBackend  | Fetch file by ID\n\nYour backend should treat `file_0` as:\n    \n    `const fileId = params.file_0\n    `\n\nand never expect `download_url` from the model.\n\n## Why this exists (historical reason)\n\nInternally, OpenAI:\n\n  * stores files in a secure registry\n  * does not want models fabricating URLs\n  * guarantees file access via ID only\n\nSo file params are lowered to IDs at the model boundary.\n\n* * *\n\n## Final verdict\n\nImage: :check_mark: You are sending the tool definition correctly\nImage: :check_mark: The UI schema is correct\nImage: :check_mark: The widget schema is correct\nImage: :check_mark: The platform is intentionally rewriting file params\nImage: :check_mark: The reported `string | null` type is expected\n\nJaime_Cham January 17, 2026, 2:39am 2\n\nFurther follow up, where I made it accept either the string or the object, has me seeing that ChatGPT doesn’t send either! Instead of a file_id, it tries to send a local path, like `/mnt/data/best run niseko (CUT).mp4`, which is a path to a file in the built-in container, and completely unaccesible.\n\nHelp!"
